        "notes": "RIGOR. VERIFIED CORRECT (all recomputed): d = n! = 2, 6, 24, 120 for n = 2..5; rank G = C_n = 2, 5, 14, 42 (Catalan); corank 0, 1, 10, 78 with the stated sum-of-squares decompositions exact \u2014 10 = 3^2 + 1^2 is dim[2,1,1]^2 + dim[1^4]^2, and 78 = 6^2+5^2+4^2+1^2 is dim[3,1,1]^2 + dim[2,2,1]^2 + dim[2,1,1,1]^2 + dim[1^5]^2, i.e. exactly the l(lambda) >= 3 partitions the note says convention D kills; dim TL_n = C_n confirms the quotient claim. The tabled optimum sums to 0.998 - 0.010 + 0.009 + 0.046 - 0.044 + 0 = 0.999, consistent with the net constraint at n_target approx 1.000. INTERNAL CONTRADICTION (reported, not fixed): the n = 3 paragraph says 'the Borromean link B_3 as the unique LP vertex', and the sibling rem:operator-selection-lp-interpretation opens 'selects the Borromean link B_3 as the unique optimizer over the 6-element NF basis of H_3(q)' \u2014 but the correction blockquote FOUR LINES BELOW, in this same file, states that on H_n the LP 'has a corank G-dimensional space of optima differing by radical directions the trace cannot see', and it tabulates corank = 1 at n = 3. Uniqueness therefore holds only on the quotient H_3/rad tr_M = TL_3, not on the 6-element NF basis both sentences name. Proposed: qualify both 'unique' claims with 'on the quotient', or state the one-parameter radical family explicitly."
